# LAYERS -- a teaching track. Four bars of the same phrase, six times, with
# one more voice added each pass.
#
# Play it through once and you have heard the whole argument of this library:
# nothing here gets louder or more complicated, and by the last section it
# sounds like a band. That is layering, and it is the difference between a
# score that sounds like a test tone and one that sounds like music.
#
#   bare    one 25% pulse. A tune, and nothing else.
#   body    + the same line on a triangle an octave down. THE single biggest
#           improvement in the file, and it costs one voice.
#   ground  + a bass alternating root and octave. Now there is a floor.
#   colour  + an arpeggio. One voice cycling a chord: harmony for the price
#           of a melody line.
#   pulse   + drums. Time, which the ear was supplying for itself until now.
#   air     + a counter-line a sixth above the lead. The top of the mix.
#
# `sound render layers --era 8bit` and `--era 16bit` are the same six steps on
# two different rigs.

@track layers
  name   LAYERS
  bpm    116
  beats  16
  era    8bit
  tags   demo teaching

@section bare
@voice lead inst=pulse25 mix=0.20
  a4  .   .   .   c5  .   .   .   e5  .   .   .   d5  .   .   .
  c5  .   .   .   a4  .   .   .   b4  .   .   .   .   .   .   .
  f4  .   .   .   a4  .   .   .   c5  .   .   .   b4  .   .   .
  a4  .   .   .   g4  .   .   .   a4  .   .   .   .   .   .   .

